# Core
#
# DATABASE_URL serves runtime queries; DIRECT_URL serves schema operations
# (migrate deploy / db push / db execute — see `directUrl` in
# prisma/schema.prisma). Migrations need a connection of their own: over a
# pooled one the _prisma_migrations completion write has twice gone missing,
# leaving "failed" rows that P3009-poison every later deploy.
#
# On Supabase, set BOTH to the Supavisor **session** pooler (port 5432), which
# you'll find under Connect → Session pooler:
#
#   postgresql://postgres.<ref>:<password>@aws-1-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres
#
# Two traps that make the other strings the wrong answer here:
#   - the *transaction* pooler (port 6543) multiplexes connections and cannot
#     keep prepared statements, which Prisma needs;
#   - the *direct* host (db.<ref>.supabase.co) resolves to IPv6 only, and the
#     deploy VM has no global IPv6 address, so it is simply unreachable there.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:5432/DATABASE?sslmode=require"
DIRECT_URL="postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:5432/DATABASE?sslmode=require"
AUTH_SECRET="generate-with-openssl-rand-base64-32"
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=""
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# Credential recovery email (required for “Forgot your password?”)
RESEND_API_KEY=""
EMAIL_FROM="Juno <hello@your-verified-domain.example>"

# Optional Google sign-in
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=""
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=""

# Optional billing
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=""
STRIPE_PRICE_PRO=""
STRIPE_PRICE_MAX=""
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=""

# Optional S3-compatible uploads
S3_BUCKET=""
S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=""
S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=""
S3_REGION="us-east-1"
S3_ENDPOINT=""
S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE="false"
S3_PUBLIC_URL=""

# Universal managed app connectors (Composio).
# Without this the Connections page shows only Juno's built-in connectors; the
# Gmail/Slack/Linear/… directory stays empty. Free key: platform.composio.dev
COMPOSIO_API_KEY=""

# Deep research + web search (Tavily). REQUIRED for the composer's "Deep
# research" toggle to appear — it plans sub-questions, searches, and reads pages.
# Free tier is enough. Without it, Anthropic/Google/xAI models can still use
# their OWN native web search via the plain "Search" toggle.
TAVILY_API_KEY=""

# Voice. WITHOUT these two providers set, Juno silently falls back to the
# browser's Web Speech API — i.e. the macOS system voice, which reads French
# with an English accent and mis-transcribes non-English dictation.
STT_PROVIDER="openai"          # "openai" | "deepgram"
TTS_PROVIDER="openai"          # "openai" | "elevenlabs"
OPENAI_API_KEY=""              # also enables the GPT models + realtime voice relay
# Optional overrides (defaults shown):
# STT_MODEL="gpt-4o-transcribe"   # far better than whisper-1 on French
# TTS_MODEL="gpt-4o-mini-tts"     # reads each language with a native accent
# TTS_VOICE="alloy"
# DEEPGRAM_API_KEY=""
# ELEVENLABS_API_KEY=""
# ELEVENLABS_VOICE_ID=""

# Modal. Every other provider needs only its API key; Modal needs all THREE of
# these or the provider stays hidden, because auth is a proxy-token pair rather
# than a bearer key and the endpoint hosts are named after your workspace.
#   modal workspace proxy-tokens create   -> Modal-Key wk-… / Modal-Secret ws-…
#   modal endpoint create --model google/gemma-4-31b-it
#   modal endpoint list                   -> deployment names
# Each model is its OWN endpoint, so URLs are derived per model from
# MODAL_WORKSPACE plus the `endpoint` name in the catalog (src/lib/models.ts) —
# there is no single base URL to set. Add a model = one catalog line.
#
# COST: a "Managed" endpoint (Kimi K3) is Modal's shared capacity billed per
# token. Every other endpoint is dedicated GPUs billed per second FOR AS LONG AS
# IT IS LIVE, which the $30/mo Starter credit does not absorb for long. Stop the
# ones you aren't using: `modal endpoint stop <name>`.
# MODAL_WORKSPACE=""
# MODAL_KEY=""
# MODAL_SECRET=""
# MODAL_REGION=""                # optional, defaults to us-west
# MODAL_BASE_URL=""              # optional, overrides derivation for odd hosts
